      "story": [
        "The title <b>Leben und leben lassen</b> means live and let live. On the ward, <b>lassen</b> does two jobs.",
        "<b>Let:</b> <b>Wir lassen den Patienten schlafen. Lassen Sie den Patienten schlafen.</b> The patient is the accusative object: <b>den Patienten</b>. <b>der Patient</b> is nominative. <b>dem Patienten</b> is the dative receiver from chapter 21, still correct for that other job. <b>den Patient</b> is missing -en.",
        "<b>Have something done:</b> <b>Wir lassen das Blut abnehmen.</b> Someone else takes the blood. <b>abnehmen</b> is one word. The active stays correct: <b>Die Pflegekraft nimmt das Blut ab.</b> <b>Wir lassen Blut abnehmen</b>, without das, is also correct. <b>Wir lassen die Wunde reinigen</b> means we have the wound cleaned. <b>Wir reinigen die Wunde</b> means we clean it ourselves, and that stays correct.",
        "The second verb is a <b>bare infinitive</b> at the end. No <b>zu</b>. <b>zu schlafen</b> and <b>abzunehmen</b> belong to the zu-infinitive in chapter 68. Chapter 69 sets lassen next to the modals and bleiben. Not <b>schläft</b> after lassen.",
        "Persons: <b>ich lasse, du lässt, er/sie lässt, wir lassen, ihr lasst, sie/Sie lassen.</b> She <b>lässt</b>. Formal Sie <b>lassen</b>. Imperative: <b>lass, lasst, lassen Sie.</b> <b>Lass Sie</b> mixes the du form with formal Sie.",
        "<b>Die Wunde wird gereinigt</b> is the present passive, chapter 57, and stays correct. <b>Die Wunde lässt sich reinigen</b> means it can be cleaned. That is a third pattern, and this chart does not drill it. The usual perfect is <b>hat schlafen lassen</b>. <b>schlafen gelassen</b> is also heard. Neither perfect is drilled here."
      ],
